      "title": "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "29a5cb5b-86c2-5918-bb8d-8e604a680231",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,2),(1,0),$ and $(-1,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=2(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,0)}=2(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}=2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-2)}=2(-1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172038/workspace/evidence/visual-repair/assets/bc1e80022d23b99b2a2821469301d5741aea411790600473890c7ecf982406b8.png)",
